is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Current state is healthy; I cannot reproduce a gateway outage on either floor. The “gateway boxes” for both affected subnets are SVIs on the device sshswitch: br_v5 (10.5.0.254/24) and br_v6 (10.6.0.254/24). From floor hosts we have end-to-end reachability to these gateways: pc1 -> 10.5.0.254 and pc2/dc_pc5 -> 10.5.0.254 all succeed with 0% loss and ~0.5 ms RTT; pc3/pc4 -> 10.6.0.254 also succeed with 0% loss and ~0.5–0.6 ms RTT. On sshswitch, the gateway interfaces are up with correct IPs (br_v5 10.5.0.254, br_v6 10.6.0.254) and their VLAN subinterfaces (eth0.5, eth0.6 and bond0.5, bond0.6) are present and enslaved to the right bridges. The L2 path from users to the gateways is correct: switch4 has access ports for VLAN 5 (eth1/2/3) in br_vlan5 and for VLAN 6 (eth4/5) in br_vlan6, with the trunk toward sshswitch on eth0 carrying VLANs 5 and 6 (eth0.5/eth0.6 up).

Mechanism-wise, if those SVIs were down after a reload you would see ARP failures and gateway pings drop. Given everything is now up and reachable, the reported outage was likely transient during/just after the reload while bridge/subinterfaces were initializing.

Root cause: No persistent fault found; users were impacted during the reload window before sshswitch’s VLAN bridges/SVIs were fully up.

Corrective fix: No changes required now. To prevent recurrence, ensure sshswitch’s bridge/VLAN subinterfaces are enabled to auto-start on boot and consider a post-reload health check before declaring the network back in service.
=== END ===